{"ticket_id": "marshmallow-1384", "status": "attempted", "resolved": false, "regressions": [], "fail_to_pass": [], "error": "Coder produced no source changes", "coder_attempts": 3, "coder_tool_calls": 60, "coder_tokens": 1266347, "tester_tool_calls": 12, "tester_tokens": 113561, "tester_reproduce_attempts": 2, "planner_tool_calls": 15, "planner_tokens": 153660, "internal_verdict": "failed", "regressed_mid_loop": [], "suite_broken_reason": null, "calibration": null, "judge_ran": true, "judge_decision": "escalate", "judge_instruction": "[judge output did not start with RETRY/ESCALATE -- defaulting to escalate] To solve this issue, we need to ensure that when pre-instantiated schema instances (or lists of them) are nested via `fields.Nested`, any dotted `only` or `exclude` options (as well as `load_only` and `dump_only`) specified on the parent schema are correctly propagated and intersected/unioned with the nested schema instance's own options.\n\n### Root Cause\n1. When a schema class is passed to `fields.Nested` (e.g. `fields.Nested(Child)`), marshmallow instantiates it dynamically inside the `Nested.schema` property, passing `only`, `exclude`, `load_only`, and `dump_only` derived from `_nested_normalized_option()`.\n2. When a schema instance is passed directly (e.g. `fields.Nested(Child(only=(...)))`), marshmallow simply returns the pre-existing instance (`self.nested`) directly from `Nested.schema` without applying the parent schema's dotted options (`only`, `exclude`, etc.) defined at the parent level.\n3. Furthermore, schema normalization (`_normalize_nested_options`) looks up parent fields via `self.declared_fields[key]`. For container fields like `fields.List` or `fields.Dict`, `self.declared_fields[key]` is the container field itself, so options need to be correctly handled both at the `Nested` field level and when normalizing options on the Schema.\n\n### Fix\nIn `src/marshmallow/fields.py`, within the `Nested.schema` property, when `isinstance(self.nested, SchemaABC)` is true, we should update the schema instance's `only` and `exclude` attributes (by intersecting/combining them with any dotted options from `self._nested_normalized_option(\"only\")` and `self._nested_normalized_option(\"exclude\")`), just as schema classes do upon instantiation.", "guided_round_edit_calls": null, "escalated": true, "elapsed_seconds": 723.7}
